1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,650
One torpedo, one ship.

2
00:00:01,670 --> 00:00:09,280
By this rule Otto Kretschmer became, measured by tonnage sunk, the most successful U-boat commander of the Second World War,

3
00:00:09,310 --> 00:00:13,080
and yet his war was already over in March 1941.

4
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:20,410
With U-99 he attacked convoys mostly at night, surfaced and often right between the columns of merchant ships.

5
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:26,950
Instead of firing several torpedoes at once, he got so close that a single shot was usually enough.

6
00:00:27,060 --> 00:00:32,070
Because he rarely sent radio messages on patrol, he was called Silent Otto.

7
00:00:32,090 --> 00:00:38,720
In a single night in October 1940 he sank seven ships of convoy SC 7.

8
00:00:38,740 --> 00:00:44,760
A little over two weeks later, in one night, the British armed merchant cruisers Laurentic and Patroclus were hit.

9
00:00:44,780 --> 00:00:52,040
In total he is credited with 47 ships sunk, about 274,000 gross register tons.

10
00:00:52,060 --> 00:00:55,120
Hundreds of sailors died on these ships.

11
00:00:55,140 --> 00:01:00,160
In March 1941 he attacked convoy HX 112.

12
00:01:00,180 --> 00:01:05,390
On the same night the German navy also lost Joachim Schepke and his boat U-100.

13
00:01:05,410 --> 00:01:10,690
Depth charges from the British destroyer Walker forced U-99 to the surface.

14
00:01:10,710 --> 00:01:12,840
Kretschmer had the boat scuttled.

15
00:01:12,860 --> 00:01:16,940
Three men died; 40 were rescued and taken prisoner.

16
00:01:16,960 --> 00:01:22,000
The British commander Donald Macintyre kept Kretschmer's binoculars as a souvenir.

17
00:01:22,020 --> 00:01:26,230
Kretschmer was later sent to a camp in Canada, where he planned an escape.

18
00:01:26,250 --> 00:01:30,050
He and three other officers were to escape through a tunnel.

19
00:01:30,070 --> 00:01:34,020
A German U-boat was to pick them up off the coast of New Brunswick.

20
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:38,780
But the Canadians were reading the coded letters and waited there with warships.

21
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:42,010
The escape failed, and the U-boat barely got away.

22
00:01:42,140 --> 00:01:45,550
Kretschmer remained a prisoner until 1947.

23
00:01:45,570 --> 00:01:50,950
He later served in the West German navy and with NATO, finally as a flotilla admiral.

24
00:01:50,970 --> 00:02:00,270
He died in 1998, not at sea, but after a fall during a boat trip on the Danube while celebrating his golden wedding anniversary with his wife.
